---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevFormatMarkdownTable from '@/components/tools/data/DevFormatMarkdownTable.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="markdown-table-to-csv">
  <DevFormatMarkdownTable client:load mode="md-to-csv" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>What counts as a Markdown table</h2>
    <p>
      A Markdown table needs three parts, in order: a header row of pipe-separated cells, a separator
      row where each cell is dashes with optional colons, and zero or more body rows. Leading and
      trailing pipes are optional, and both styles work. If the separator is missing, or its cell count
      does not match the header, the parser tells you and does not guess, because a wrong guess would
      shift all columns.
    </p>
    <pre><code>| Endpoint   | Method | p95 latency |
| ---------- | :----: | ----------: |
| /v1/orders | GET    | 84 ms       |</code></pre>

    <h2>What happens to Markdown-specific syntax</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Escaped pipes (<code>\|</code>) become a literal pipe in the CSV field, correctly quoted so a spreadsheet does not split on it.</li>
      <li>Alignment markers are read and then discarded. CSV has no alignment. A spreadsheet applies its own from the value type.</li>
      <li>Padding spaces are trimmed from each cell.</li>
      <li>Inline formatting (<code>**bold**</code>, backticks, link syntax) stays as literal text, because removing it would lose the URL from a link. For plain values, remove it in the grid.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;br&gt;</code> tags stay as text and do not become newlines. A CSV field can legally contain a real line break, but an automatic change would alter your data. Make that change in the grid if you need it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Opening the result in Excel</h2>
    <p>
      Excel splits a CSV with your regional list separator, not from the file&rsquo;s contents. In a
      locale where the decimal separator is a comma (most of continental Europe and Latin America), a
      comma-delimited file opens with each row in column A.
    </p>
    <p>Three fixes, easiest first:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Switch the delimiter to <strong>semicolon</strong> in the output options before downloading. Excel in those locales opens it correctly.</li>
      <li>In Excel, use <strong>Data → From Text/CSV</strong> and do not double-click the file. Choose the delimiter in the import dialog.</li>
      <li>Rename the file to <code>.txt</code>, which forces Excel to show the import wizard.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Google Sheets and Numbers detect the delimiter themselves and usually read both correctly.
    </p>

    <h2>JSON instead of CSV</h2>
    <p>
      Switch the output format to JSON and each body row becomes an object keyed by the header cells.
      This is the fastest way from a documentation table to a test fixture, a seed file or a lookup
      map. A table of HTTP codes, currency codes, feature flags or config defaults becomes usable data
      in one step.
    </p>

    <h2>Round-tripping</h2>
    <p>
      Markdown table to CSV and back keeps the data but not the presentation: alignment markers and
      padding come from your new choices. To keep alignment through a round trip, note it before you
      convert, or edit the CSV and go back through <a href="/csv-to-markdown-table">CSV to Markdown</a>
      and set the alignment again in the grid.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
